🔴 Vulnerability Title

Hardcoded Database Credentials in Configuration File

🔴 Description

It was observed that the application stores database credentials, including username and password, in plaintext within the DBAccessInfo.xml configuration file. These credentials are accessible through a shared network path used by the Excel add-in. The file exposes sensitive authentication details without encryption or protection. This indicates insecure credential management and improper handling of sensitive data.

🔴 Impact
Attackers can directly use exposed credentials to access backend databases
Leads to unauthorized data access, modification, or deletion
May result in full compromise of application and connected systems
🔧 Mitigation
It is recommended to implement secure credential storage using a centralized secrets management solution (e.g., vault or encrypted store)
Avoid storing plaintext credentials in configuration files and use secure authentication methods such as integrated authentication or tokens
Restrict access to configuration files and enforce encryption for sensitive data at rest
📌 PoC Steps

Step 1: Navigate to shared path containing configuration files
Step 2: Open DBAccessInfo.xml
Step 3: Locate <Username> and <Password> fields
Step 4: Observe plaintext credentials exposed

